<div class="prose max-w-prose">

As lead product designer at Buzuci, I designed a wide range of personalised
products for clients across many different contexts. This included everything
from individual mug designs created as personal gifts to large-scale corporate
orders that needed to be consistent, high-quality, and production-ready.

The work required more than just creating visually appealing artwork. Every
design had to be print-ready and adaptable across multiple materials, finishes,
and manufacturing processes. The same idea might need to work across different
machines, departments, and production methods depending on the final product.

## Designing for real-world production

A major part of the role was translating design concepts into work that could be
produced reliably in a real manufacturing environment. That meant considering
how each design would behave across different applications, including:

- Laser Cutting and Engraving
- Sublimation Printing
- Vinyl Print and Cut
- DTF (Direct-to-Film) Printing
- UV Printing
- Textile Printing
- Resin Dome Prints

Each process has its own technical constraints, material behaviours, and layout
requirements. A design that works beautifully for one method may need to be
adjusted significantly for another. I had to think carefully about scale,
linework, colour separation, material compatibility, and final finish to ensure
that each design remained true to the original concept while being practical to
produce.

## Cross-department collaboration and multilingual communication

Because these products touched multiple production teams, collaboration was a
core part of the role. I worked closely with different departments to understand
how each design would be executed, what each machine could handle, and what
needed to be adapted before production began.

This also involved working in a multilingual environment, communicating with both
English- and Afrikaans-speaking clients and colleagues. That required clear,
practical communication, strong attention to detail, and the ability to explain
technical requirements in a way that worked across different contexts and
backgrounds.

This required strong communication and a practical understanding of how creative
ideas translate into production workflows. It also meant making design choices
with real-world implications in mind — balancing aesthetics with manufacturability,
turnaround time, and production quality.

## The result

This role strengthened my understanding of design as both a creative and
production-led discipline. I was responsible for creating work that looked
appealing, functioned well in context, and could be manufactured consistently
across a variety of formats and materials.

It was a role that required both artistic thinking and operational awareness,
helping me develop a more complete approach to product design — one that connects
creative intent with production realities.

